## Problem

Proof that a fix works in the real app is hand-built for every fix: boot the app, seed state, click, capture, upload. That takes most of an hour and is where most automated fix attempts die.

## What this adds

Scenarios: ordinary [e2e](https://e2e.com) tests in `scripts/scenarios/*.e2e.ts` that run against the real dev stack (real server, HTTP, WebSocket) with the dev replay agent as the model, in a real browser on the web client or on a pooled iPhone simulator running the mobile app. e2e is the runner; there is no custom one.

```ts
test("Tab moves from the add-project Git URL field to its submit button", WEB, async ({ t3, app, screen, browser }) => {
  await t3.project("demo");
  await t3.open();

  await screen.getByRole("button", "Add project").tap();
  await screen.getByText("Git URL").tap();
  await screen.getByRole("combobox").fill("https://github.com/pingdotgg/t3code.git");
  const submit = screen.getByRole("button", "Continue (Enter)");
  await expect(submit).toBeEnabled();

  await browser.keyboard.press("Tab");
  await app.screenshot("after-tab");
  await expect(submit).toBeFocused();
});
```

- `vp run scenarios` runs them. e2e starts the app on a free port, drives the browser, and writes `report.json`, `summary.md`, a trace, screenshots and a video per test. Both scenarios run in under 30 seconds, app boot included.
- `vp run scenarios:prove  --base  --head ` runs `e2e run` for that scenario on both commits and reports `failsOnBase`, `passesOnHead`,  [...]

